#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Assemble the Ordinary of the Mass for the browser, under its rights.

Usage: mass-ordinary {check,structure,coverage} [--calendar NAME] ...

Why the Ordinary is not in the calendars
----------------------------------------
`src/sources/calendars/<calendar>/propers.yaml` is a `sections -> masses ->
propers` index and says so of itself: the postconciliar one states that "the
invariant Ordinary is out of scope". Forcing the Ordinary in as a pseudo-mass
would put a celebration with no date into the sanctoral spine, count it into
the propers census totals that two publications carry, and make those totals
lie. (Sibling tool ids are deliberately not written out here: tmt reads a bare
one as an undeclared dependency, and this tool invokes none of them.) The Ordinary is not a formulary. It is the frame the formularies are poured
into, and it gets its own source and its own file.

Two objects, and only one of them holds words
---------------------------------------------
The book's own words live in an artifact under the edition that prints them ---
a TSV, one physical line per printed block, with a hash and a rights record.
The inventory under `src/sources/inventories/<calendar>-ordo-missae-v1.toml`
holds only the *order of sections*, the witnesses, and what is absent and why.
It never restates a line of text that an artifact already holds. That is the
lesson the propers side learned expensively: a hand-typed second copy of an
artifact drifted from it at 45 loci, and every error the site served sat inside
those 45.

Where a rite has no text this surface may carry, the inventory preserves the
element skeleton, with each element's received Latin incipit as its identity and
an `absent` key naming the reason. The reason distinguishes an unavailable
witness or exemplar from a rights restriction, a model that spans independently
sourceable parts, material belonging to another layer, and a structural element
to which fixed text does not apply. An incipit is a reference, and a reference
is a fact. The prayer is not reproduced.

Those reasons are not one reason. The postconciliar Latin side distinguishes the
reform's own withheld matter, sourceable antecedent material not yet carried,
the absence of an antecedent witness, coarse model spans, and structural or
outside-layer slots. Its English side separately distinguishes registered
official exemplars not carried on this surface, proper texts outside the
Ordinary, elements with no fixed text, composite spans, and quiet prayers for
which no exact exemplar is held. A generic rightsholder key over those cases
would turn unlike facts into one false claim.

Two languages, one rule, and the relation that keeps them honest
---------------------------------------------------------------
An element of an inventory-declared section may name a text in either language,
and `english` and `latin` are the same object under two names: a witness, an
artifact that witness lists, one or more of that artifact's elements, and the
column they stand in. `side_text` resolves both, once, so that the two sides
cannot drift into two rules --- which is exactly what had already happened
between `from_artifact` and `from_inventory`, and is why the postconciliar Latin
could not be reached at all however much of it this project held.

Two languages do not imply one provenance. The artifact-backed Roman frames join
the facing columns of one printing and may therefore assume only a shared key
space. Each carried column still declares an `english` or `latin` provenance
block with its own explicit relation and collation state. Inventory-declared
elements may instead join languages from different books; `side_text` validates
each language block independently.

That difference is a claim about identity, and an unlabelled claim is the failure
this layer exists to prevent. A reader looking at a page headed `Missale Romanum,
editio typica tertia` and seeing Latin under an element will take it for that
book's Latin unless told otherwise AT THAT ELEMENT. So a block that draws on a
book other than the one the frame names must declare `relation = "antecedent"`,
and then two further things are required of it and refused without:

  `collation`  --- `collated` where this repository holds a finding, cited, that
                   states the relationship between this element's text and the
                   controlling edition's; `uncollated` where it does not. Both
                   may be carried and they must not look the same. The propers
                   side learned the cost of running them together.
  `note`       --- one paragraph, per element, carried onto the translation row
                   and therefore beside the words rather than at the foot of a
                   page: whose words these are, at which leaf and printed page,
                   which accidents of that printing they carry, and the sentence
                   that the frame does not carry the controlling edition's text
                   on this surface and this is not it. A relation nobody can
                   check is a claim nobody made.

`collation = "collated"` additionally requires `collation_finding`, a citation to
the tracked finding. Without it the grade is an assertion, and the whole point of
having two grades is that one of them is not.

What is NOT modelled here, deliberately: the witness's own spelling is never
normalised toward the target's. The 1861 column prints `justum`, `coelis`,
`Jesu`, the ae- and oe-ligatures, and its own misprints --- `nodie` for `hodie`
at In spiritu humilitatis. Normalising them would manufacture a witness to a book
the selected artifact row does not witness; the note names them instead.

The publication boundary
------------------------
This generated corpus publishes only a witness whose `rights` is
`public-domain` or `project-created`. A licence or permission scoped to some
other surface is source-study metadata, not a softer public-domain state, and
it cannot enter the witness table or a text join here.

A `[[restricted_witnesses]]` row is different again: source-only provenance,
not a text offer.  ICEL's prescribed excerpt acknowledgement and the source
library's completed text-free quarantine remain checkable there, but neither
the witness metadata nor any text, source id, rights state, or turns from it
enter the generated translation manifest.  An element that tries to join a
restricted witness is refused rather than silently made available.

What this tool refuses to do
----------------------------
*It does not decide what may be published.* A witness's `rights` is read from
the inventory and cross-checked against the source library's own record for the
same id; a disagreement is a hard failure rather than a resolved one.

*It does not fill a gap from a neighbour.* An element with no text in the chosen
witness says so. Substituting another translator's words is the confusion a
choice of witness exists to prevent.

*It does not flatten two sections' key spaces into one.* An artifact's element
keys are unique inside that artifact and nowhere else --- three of them recur
across the 1861 Ordinary's six sections, because the book really does say
`Gloria Patri` twice. Emitted keys are therefore `<section>/<element>`, which is
unique by construction and stays readable.

*It does not rank the Eucharistic Prayers.* The postconciliar Missal prints
four and the priest chooses; the default is the first the Missal itself prints,
which is the only order this project can state without preferring one prayer.

Slots: where a proper of the day sits in the frame
--------------------------------------------------
The Ordinary is the frame and the propers are poured into it, and *where* each
proper is poured is liturgical fact --- the same fact for every page that ever
draws this frame. So it is declared in the inventory, beside the elements it
seats propers among, and never worked out by a renderer. A slot names one
element, whether the proper stands `before` or `after` it, the proper names it
receives, and the `locus` in the missal that puts it there.

Three things are checked here rather than trusted. A slot's anchor must be an
element this file really emits, so renaming an element breaks the build instead
of silently unseating a proper. A proper name may be claimed by one slot only,
because two claims are an ambiguity a page would have to resolve by guessing.
And the slots must run forward through the elements in the order they are
declared, which makes the file's own order readable as the order of the rite
and catches a slot moved without its neighbours.

What is NOT declared is as deliberate as what is. A proper the missal does not
place --- an Ember Saturday's later chants, the whole of Good Friday --- gets no
slot, and a page must show it in the missal's own order rather than seat it
somewhere plausible. That is the refusal this layer owes: a seat invented for a
proper resolves cleanly, reads correctly, and is wrong.
"""
